  <p class="foot"><b>A note on Hillman.</b> He holds a Classics PhD from Wisconsin with a specialty in ancient pharmacy and bacteriology, and <em>The Chemical Muse</em> was published by St. Martin's. He reads the Greek as pharmacology first, with no theology between the reader and the text, and that lens opened this page. The Amazon sources turn out to be unusually hospitable to it.</p>
  <MethodLink class="foot" />
  <div class="srcs">
    <p><a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Hippocrates/airwatpl.17.17.html">Hippocrates, Airs, Waters, Places 17 (Adams trans.)</a></p>
    <p><a href="https://sententiaeantiquae.com/tag/amazons/">Sententiae Antiquae: Sauromatae and Mimnermus fr. 21, with Greek</a></p>
